1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,000
This is StreamCart: a small, movable discovery overlay that keeps the Twitch stream in view.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Open the capsule to see a real Channel3 product, why it appeared, and where to explore the full collection.

3
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:58,000
The broadcaster sets the live topic in Twitch chat. Signed EventSub carries that command to StreamCart.

4
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Channel3 finds candidates; the broadcaster decides. Approval changes the visible provenance and is authorized by Twitch's signed Extension token.

5
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:38,000
A second test account chooses when to save interest. The event stores channel, product, and action—not a viewer ID or profile.

6
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:58,000
The full Twitch collection comes from Channel3. Every brand in its response becomes a quick catalogue filter.

7
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:16,000
SafeSearch starts on. Turning it off broadens ambiguous results, while the server still blocks its mandatory weapon categories.

8
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Counts represent actions, not unique viewers. Small totals stay hidden and larger totals are grouped; this is not a guarantee of anonymity.

9
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Codex built and validated this flow. The proof slate ties the Hosted Test, API checks, and implementation back to this session.

10
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
StreamCart: creator-controlled discovery, while Twitch stays in view.
